<<~ ahu #/head>>

! CapabilityProvider ~ what the gate answers, and what it does not

This interface carries Keyhive's ''binary'' access gate — `read` or `admin`, cryptographic, one
Keyhive Document per Lararium bag URL. That names the whole of what any code here checks.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-axis-that-does-not-gate>>

!! The access axis reads as a MODEL

`pull · read · edit · admin` mirrors Keyhive's own verbs one-for-one, and ''no call site in this tree
narrows an action by verb.'' `CapabilityAccess` admits two values everywhere it appears.

''Naming the axis here as a checked tier would promise a reader a gate they could then fail to find.''
The model stands as doctrine at `lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/causal-islands`, where a reader meets
it for what it does.

Outside the model entirely: `promote` and `propose` name a CEREMONY, `sync` names pull-at-scale, and
`revoke` names an admin operation. None of the three sits on the ladder.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/law>>

!! The invariants

# ''Provider-shaped, so implementations swap.'' KeyhiveProvider wraps the WASM; a no-op provider
serves tests with no crypto; a Beelay-shaped provider lands when upstream settles. The interface
moves for none of them.
# ''One Keyhive Document per bag URL.'' `registerBag` mints or looks up, and the doc id it returns
names the gate's subject.
# ''A JOINEE MUST restore from its archive.'' Keyhive generates prekeys per init, so a peer that
re-initialized fresh would lose the contact card that membership sealed to, and stop decrypting after
a reboot. Restoring carries the prekey SECRETS and the stable card forward. ''Those bytes hold raw
secret material and the caller persists them ENCRYPTED-AT-REST.''
# ''A contact card precedes a delegation.'' The card carries the prekeys and identity proof another
peer needs before it can stand as the audience of one.
# ''A delegation ships as BYTES.'' `delegate` returns both a stable id for later revocation and the
serialized events the audience must ingest — the federation transport carries them, and nothing
implicit reaches the other peer.
# ''A CROSSING IS READ, NEVER LABELLED.'' `eventsForPeer` hands bare bytes, so the sender's variant and
island do not ride the wire — and they do not need to. The receiver parses those same bytes back into
events and its OWN handler stamps the truth, so an admitted vessel's record is typed because it TOOK the
events. `absorbCapEvents` is that order — ingest, settle the handler's writes, then persist whatever
keyhive refused — and the order is load-bearing: a store keeps the FIRST writer's variant, so a record
written ahead of the ingest masks a knowable one for good. ''The read may fail; the record may not'' —
keyhive refuses an unreadable bundle whole, and the events persist anyway for a later boot to replay.
# ''Boot replay stays TORN-TOLERANT.'' `hydrateFromEventStore` counts what a corrupt record forced it
to skip; a skipped cap-event degrades a membership slice and never brings down the boot.
# ''Boot loads the SELF slice eagerly and defers the rest.'' Passing the vessel's own sentinel doc-ids
loads the self slice plus cross-cutting events; every held or foreign island defers. Absent that list
everything loads eagerly, which stands as the single-daemon default rather than the general case.
# ''Materialization stays idempotent.'' Pulling one deferred island's events noops where the island
already sits resident, so an access shore may call it every time without cost.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

